%[text] # Introduction to Projectile Motion
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
%[text] Modeling projectile motion for a point mass is relatively easy, so it's a good starting point to review working in MATLAB and Simulink. First, we have to define our system. Let $m${"altText":"m"} be the mass of our point in kilograms, $g = 9.81${"altText":"g = 9.81"} be the acceleration due to gravity in meters per second squared, and $p${"altText":"p"} be the position of our mass at time $t${"altText":"t"}. Remember that the Newtonian equations of motion state that the sum of all forces on our object is equal to the mass times the acceleration of the object, that is $\\displaystyle m\\frac{d^2p}{dt^2} = \\sum\_i F\_i${"altText":"m(d^2p)\/(dt^2) = sum over i of F\_i"}.
%[text] ## Free Fall
%[text] The simplest projectile motion problem is to throw our point mass directly upwards, with a magnitude of $v\_0${"altText":"v\_0"} meters per second. In this case, we know that only a single force, the downward pull of gravity, acts on our mass. Therefore, to solve the equation for a position, $p(t)${"altText":"p(t)"}, as a function of time, we need to know the initial velocity, $v\_0${"altText":"v\_0"}, and the initial position, $x\_0${"altText":"x\_0"}, of our mass.
%[text] Putting this together, the differential equation describing our model becomes
%[text]{"align":"center"} $m\\frac{d^2p}{dt^2} = -mg${"altText":"m(d^2p)\/(dt^2) = -mg"} with the initial conditions $\\left.\\frac{dp}{dt}\\right|\_{t=0} = v\_0${"altText":"dp\/dt evaluated at t=0 = v\_0"} and $p(0) = p\_0${"altText":"p(0) = p\_0"}.
%[text] Rather than using equations from calculus to solve for $p${"altText":"p"}, let's simulate the position of our mass step-by-step. 
%[text] ### Numerical Approximation
%[text] First, we define our spatial coordinate system. Let's assume that our point mass starts at the origin. We define "upwards" to be in the positive *y* direction. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:4880)
%[text] This is only a one-dimensional system for the moment. Therefore, we can treat all of our values as signed scalars.
%[text] Now, gravitational acceleration is pulling *downwards* in the $y${"altText":"y"} direction, so the acceleration of our point mass is 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $a = -g \\approx -9.81 \\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}^2}${"altText":"a = -g approximately -9.81 (m)\/(s^2)"}.
%[text] We also know that we can approximate $a${"altText":"a"} at the $i^{\\text{th}}${"altText":"i^(th)"} time step as:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $a  \\approx \\frac{v\_i - v\_{i-1}}{t\_i-t\_{i-1}}${"altText":"a approximately (v\_i - v\_(i-1))\/(t\_i-t\_(i-1))"}
%[text] where $v\_k${"altText":"v\_k"} is the velocity at time $t\_k${"altText":"t\_k"}. If our time step is consistent such that $\\text{dt} = t\_i-t\_{i-1}${"altText":"dt = t\_i-t\_(i-1)"} for any value of $i${"altText":"i"}, then we can solve this equation iteratively using [symplectic Euler](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semi-implicit_Euler_method):
%[text]{"align":"center"} $v\_i \\approx a(t\_i-t\_{i-1})+v\_{i-1} = v\_{i-1}-g\\cdot \\text{dt}.${"altText":"v\_i approximately a(t\_i-t\_(i-1))+v\_(i-1) = v\_(i-1)-g times dt."}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $v\_{i-1} \\approx v\_{i-2}-g\\cdot \\text{dt} \\Rightarrow v\_i \\approx v\_{i-2}-2g\\cdot \\text{dt}${"altText":"v\_(i-1) approximately v\_(i-2)-g times dt implies v\_i approximately v\_(i-2)-2g times dt"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\vdots${"altText":"vertical ellipsis"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $v\_i \\approx v\_0-i\\cdot g \\cdot \\text{dt}${"altText":"v\_i approximately v\_0-i times g times dt"}
%[text] We can also integrate the velocity to know the displacement (the height, $p${"altText":"p"}) at every time $t\_i${"altText":"t\_i"}. Using this semi-implicit Euler's method for first-order integration:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $p\_i \\approx p\_{i-1} + v\_i\\cdot \\text{dt}${"altText":"p\_i approximately p\_(i-1) + v\_i times dt"}.
%[text] This means that if we know $v\_0${"altText":"v\_0"}, $p\_0${"altText":"p\_0"}, and $\\text{dt}${"altText":"dt"}, we can iteratively approximate all future values of both $v\_i${"altText":"v\_i"} and $p\_i${"altText":"p\_i"} as long as our model holds. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_496b] #### Set Up Variables
m = 6;     % mass (kg)
g = 9.81;  % gravitational acceleration (m/s^2)
v0 = 10;   % initial vertical velocity (m/s)
p0 = 0;    % initial position (m)
dt = 0.01; % timestep (s)
%[text] Run this section to compute one time step of the numerical approximation. 
  %[control:button:1466]{"position":[1,2]}
vNext = v0-g*dt;        % v1 = v0+a*dt
pNext = p0+vNext*dt;    % p1 = p0+v1*dt 

tl = tiledlayout(2,1);
title(tl,"The Position of the Mass")
nexttile
v = plot([0 dt],[v0 vNext]);
ylabel("Velocity $v$ (m/s)",Interpreter="latex")
nexttile
p = plot([0 dt],[p0 pNext]);
ylabel("Displacement $p$ (m)",Interpreter="latex")
xlabel("Time (s)")
% Define a table of values
T = table(1, p0, v0, vNext, pNext, ...
    'VariableNames', {'Step', 'InitialPosition', 'InitialVelocity', 'vNext = v0-g*dt', 'pNext = p0+vNext*dt'});
disp(T)
%%
%[text] Iterate and update the plot and table:
  %[control:button:0625]{"position":[1,2]}
vPrev = vNext;   % Set up v_{i-1}
pPrev = pNext;   % Set up p_{i-1}

vNext = vPrev-g*dt;         % Compute v_i = v_{i-1}-g*dt
pNext = pPrev + vNext*dt;   % Compute p_i = p_{i-1}+v_i*dt

v.XData = [v.XData v.XData(end)+dt]; % Add another point t_i
v.YData = [v.YData vNext];           % Add another point v_i
p.XData = [p.XData p.XData(end)+dt]; % Add another point t_i
p.YData = [p.YData pNext];           % Add another point p_i
% Append new step to the table and display the result
T = [T; {T.Step(end)+1 pPrev vPrev vNext pNext}];
disp(T)
%[text] ![](text:image:5d86) **Try**. Run this section of code repeatedly. What happens to your plots? And your table?
%[text] **Note:** If you encounter errors, try going back to the [initial step](internal:M_496b) and working through the iterations again.
%%
%[text] #### How Many Timesteps?
%[text] As the motion is symmetric, launching from ground level, $p=0${"altText":"p=0"}, and assuming no air resistance, we can calculate the flight time as: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $p(t) = -\\frac{g}{2}t^2 + v\_0t${"altText":"p(t) = -(g)\/(2)t^2 + v\_0t"} 
%[text] The mass has returned to the ground when $p(t\_{\\text{end}}) = 0${"altText":"p(t\_(end)) = 0"} so solving the quadratic equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $0 = -\\frac{g}{2}t^2+v\_0t${"altText":"0 = -(g)\/(2)t^2+v\_0t"} 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\Rightarrow t = -\\frac{v\_0\\pm \\sqrt{v\_0^2}}{-g}${"altText":"implies t = -(v\_0 plus or minus sqrt(v\_0^2))\/(-g)"} 
%[text] so $t\_0=0${"altText":"t\_0=0"}, because we start at ground level, and $t\_{\\text{end}} = \\frac{2v\_0}{g}${"altText":"t\_(end) = (2v\_0)\/(g)"}.
%[text] ![](text:image:5fb8) **Try**. How many time steps, `n_timesteps`, are required to complete the full parabola from $t\_0=0${"altText":"t\_0=0"} to $t\_{\\text{end}}=\\frac{2v\_0}{g}${"altText":"t\_(end)=(2v\_0)\/(g)"}?




%[text] ![](text:image:7e0a) **Reflect**. Is `n_timesteps` required to be an integer? Why or why not? 
%%
%[text] ### Simulate and Plot in 1D
%[text] More generally, we may launch the projectile from positions other than $p=0${"altText":"p=0"}. This requires a derivation starting from $p(t) = -\\frac{g}{2}t^2 + v\_0t + p\_0${"altText":"p(t) = -(g)\/(2)t^2 + v\_0t + p\_0"} and concluding that $t\_{\\text{end}} = \\frac{v\_0+\\sqrt{v\_0^2+2gp\_0}}{g}${"altText":"t\_(end) = (v\_0+sqrt(v\_0^2+2gp\_0))\/(g)"}. Details are left as an exercise to the reader.
  %[control:button:4ae1]{"position":[1,2]}
m = 6;    % mass (kg) %[control:slider:6bca]{"position":[5,6]}
g = 9.81;                        % gravitational acceleration (m/s^2)
a = -g;                          % acceleration downwards due to gravity
v0 = 10;   % vertical velocity (m/s) %[control:slider:6337]{"position":[6,8]}
p0 = 0; % initial vertical position (m) %[control:slider:7c23]{"position":[6,7]}
dt = 0.01;       % timestep (s) %[control:dropdown:2081]{"position":[6,10]}
ShowAnimation = false;             % Choose whether or not to create an animation %[control:checkbox:6acd]{"position":[17,22]}
tEnd = (v0+sqrt(v0^2+2*g*p0))/g;
t = 0:dt:tEnd;                   % creating time array
TotTimeSteps = length(t);

v = zeros(1,TotTimeSteps); % creating empty velocity vector
p = zeros(1,TotTimeSteps); % creating empty position vector

p(1) = p0; % Initial position
v(1) = v0; % Initial velocity

for i = 2:TotTimeSteps
    v(i) = v(i-1) - g*dt;
    p(i) = p(i-1) + v(i)*dt;
end

figure
tl = tiledlayout(2,1);
title(tl,"A Parabolic Path Stopping at 0 on the Descent")
nexttile
plot(t, v)
ylabel("Velocity (m/s)")

nexttile
plot(t, p)
ylabel("Displacement y (m)")
xlabel("Time (s)")
if ShowAnimation
    figure
    pts = scatter(0,p0,"filled");
    hold on
    pLine = plot(0,p0);
    VerticalLines = round(linspace(min(p),max(p),5),1);
    for yVal = VerticalLines
        yline(yVal)
        text(1,yVal,string(yVal))
    end
    hold off
    title("Animation of Projectile Motion")
    axis off
    xlim([-1 1])              % Set window size to be convenient
    ylim([min(p)-0.1 max(p)+1])   % for the animation
    for i = 2:(TotTimeSteps)
        pts.YData = p(i);
        pLine.XData = zeros(1,i);
        pLine.YData = p(1:i);
        drawnow
    end
end
%[text] ![](text:image:2606) **Try**.
%[text] 1. Change the value of $m${"altText":"m"}. What does this do to your velocity and displacement? Why?
%[text] 2. Change the value of $v\_0${"altText":"v\_0"}. What does this do to your velocity and displacement? Why?
%[text] 3. Change the value of $dt${"altText":"dt"}. What does this do to your velocity and displacement? Why?
%[text] 4. Can you get a MATLAB error? How and why? \
