%[text] ## 2D Projectile Motion
%[text] Next, let's add some horizontal velocity. The vertical motion remains the same, but now the projectile moves in the x-direction as well. Because the directions are independent, we can solve each component of the motion separately. 
%[text] We can use some interactive controls to set this up:
v0 = 50;    % initial velocity m/s %[control:slider:2857]{"position":[6,8]}
angle = 45; % initial angle (degrees) %[control:slider:5606]{"position":[9,11]}
x0 =0;    % initial x position %[control:slider:7db0]{"position":[5,6]}
y0 =0;    % initial y position %[control:slider:712d]{"position":[5,6]}
  %[control:button:2d82]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:651e) **Exercise 1.** Use the functions `sind` and `cosd` to compute the initial vertical ($v\_{y\_0}${"altText":"v\_(y\_0)"} or `vy0`) and horizontal ($v\_{x\_0}${"altText":"v\_(x\_0)"} or `vx0`) components of the initial velocity.



%[text] ![](text:image:1220) **Exercise 2.**
%[text] Copy the 1D example and modify the code to solve the 2D problem by properly tracking and displaying both the $x${"altText":"x"} and $y${"altText":"y"} components of the parabolic path. 



%%
%[text] ## Modeling Projectiles in Simulink®
%[text] What if we now add air resistance?
%[text] The drag force depends on the velocity and thus changes at every point in time. This means that all the equations need to be written differently. Let's use Simulink to model this, since it is designed to solve differential equations. 
%[text] ### Implementing a 1D Projectile
%[text] Let's start by recreating the 1D scenario solving $\\frac{d^2p}{dt^2} = -g${"altText":"(d^2p)\/(dt^2) = -g"} with $p'(0) = 50${"altText":"p'(0) = 50"} and $p(0)=10${"altText":"p(0)=10"} in Simulink. The expectation is that you are familiar with the content in [Simulink Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/simulink-onramp/simulink). Instructions to build the model are included in the model.
open("Projectile_1D.slx")
%%
%[text] ### Adding Drag to a 1D Projectile
%[text] Recall that the drag is proportional to the square of the velocity and acts in the opposite direction. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{d^2p}{dt^2} = -g-\\text{sign}\\left(\\frac{dp}{dt}\\right)\\alpha\\left(\\frac{dp}{dt}\\right)^2${"altText":"(d^2p)\/(dt^2) = -g-sign((dp)\/(dt)) alpha ((dp)\/(dt))^2"}
%[text] where $\\alpha = \\frac{1}{2m} \\rho C\_d A${"altText":"alpha = (1)\/(2m) rho C\_d A"} with units of $\\frac{1}{\\text{meters}}${"altText":"(1)\/(meters)"} is a lumped parameter that tracks how physical characteristics of the mass impact the [drag force](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drag_coefficient) and the $-\\text{sign}\\left(\\frac{dp}{dt}\\right)${"altText":"-sign((dp)\/(dt))"} encodes the fact that drag opposes motion. For your initial solution, use $\\alpha = 0.1${"altText":"alpha = 0.1"}.
%[text] In Simulink, the RHS looks like
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Simulink block diagram showing -g - sign(dy/dt)\*alpha\*(dy/dt)^2](text:image:178b)
%[text] The five new blocks you need are:
%[text] 1. Gain 
%[text] 2. Sign
%[text] 3. Square
%[text] 4. Product
%[text] 5. Sum \
%[text] ![Simulink icon](text:image:43db) **Pro-tip.** Remember that Ctrl-R (or Cmd-R on a Mac) will rotate a block, and Ctrl-I will flip a block.
open("Projectile_1D_WithDrag.slx") %[output:75384f25]
